    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it passes along with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the E.164 national-number length rule with minimal structural impact:\n\n- **New file** `service/E164NationalLength.java` \u2014 a small, cohesive helper (one class, one static method, no surrounding complexity) holding a `country-code \u2192 required-national-digits` map (`+1` \u2192 10, `+61` \u2192 9) and throwing `IllegalArgumentException` when the national length is wrong. Unknown country codes pass through unchanged.\n- **One-line edit** to `OwnerMapper.normalizeTelephone` \u2014 after the existing E.164 range check, it delegates to `E164NationalLength.check(digits)`. This adds no new branches to the method (its cyclomatic complexity is unchanged) and touches a single line.\n\nThe rejection surfaces as **400 Bad Request** through the existing `IllegalArgumentException` handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, matching the mapper's existing E.164 failure behavior. Seed data (raw digit strings loaded directly into the DB) bypasses the mapper, so it's unaffected. The valid `04XXXXXXXX` payload normalizes to `+61` + 9 national digits and still passes.\n\nI avoided growing the large `Owner` entity class or adding branches to any existing method \u2014 the new behavior lives entirely in a fresh single-method class, so the only complexity-bearing function I authored carries no surrounding-class weight.",
